<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <!--
    No groupId or version: both are inherited, so the framework's coordinates are stated once in
    the whole repository and no two files can disagree about what this is called.
  -->
  <parent>
    <groupId>io.github.lihuu</groupId>
    <artifactId>panamaview-parent</artifactId>
    <version>0.2.0</version>
  </parent>

  <artifactId>panamaview</artifactId>
  <packaging>jar</packaging>

  <name>panamaview</name>
  <description>Drive the operating system's web view from Java over the FFM API. No JNI, no JNA,
    no bundled browser engine.</description>

  <!--
    Zero runtime dependencies on purpose: the whole point is to talk to
    Cocoa/WebKit through java.lang.foreign and nothing else.
  -->
  <dependencies/>

  <build>
    <plugins>
      <!--
        The framework's sources live here and nowhere else, and this is what says so out loud.

        Why it is worth a build step. When the framework moved out of the repository root into this
        module, any branch written before that move still adds its sources under `src/main/java/` at
        the root — `src/main/java/panamaview/windows/`, for instance. Merging such a branch produces
        **no conflict at all**: base had no file at either path, the branch adds one path, this
        module adds another, and git merges two unrelated additions silently. Maven then compiles
        this module only, so those sources are never compiled and never noticed. The build exits 0.
        Measured: four Windows source files sitting in the root, no `WindowsHost.class` anywhere, and
        nothing red.

        That is the failure this guard turns into a loud one. It is deliberately not a move: this
        module must not decide where another branch's work belongs, and copying the same sources to
        a second path makes the merge *quieter* to get wrong, not less so — both additions survive
        and the tree carries two copies nobody can see is wrong. Whoever wrote those sources owns
        moving them, and this tells them the moment their branch is built.
      -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-enforcer-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>3.4.1</version>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <id>no-source-tree-at-the-root</id>
            <phase>validate</phase>
            <goals>
              <goal>enforce</goal>
            </goals>
            <configuration>
              <rules>
                <requireFilesDontExist>
                  <message>
A source tree has appeared at the repository root (${project.basedir}/../src), but the framework's
sources live in framework/src/. Maven compiles the module, not the root, so anything under
src/main/java/ is silently never compiled — the build passes and the classes are simply absent.

This usually means a branch written before the framework moved is being merged: git reports no
conflict, because both sides only added files, and only one of those paths is on the compile path.

Move those sources into framework/src/... to match this module, then rebuild.
                  </message>
                  <files>
                    <file>${project.basedir}/../src</file>
                  </files>
                </requireFilesDontExist>
              </rules>
            </configuration>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>

      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
      </plugin>

      <!--
        No exec plugin, and no entry point: this is a library. It has no main() to run and nothing
        to launch, so declaring one would mean shipping the demo again. The demo lives in demo-app/
        and is an ordinary consumer, exactly like the templates.
      -->
    </plugins>
  </build>
</project>
